node 4_20260821-202044 codegen_edits llm_response: Looking at this kernel, it's already a cooperative tiled 3D convolution with shared-memory staging for both input and weights (structural tiling already present). The hottest loop is the compute loop, which reads `sW(cc, cin, kd, kh, kw)` where `cc` is the **slowest-varying** dimension in shared memory (stride = `Cin*K^3` = 81 floats). This means each of the 64 per-thread accumulator updates issues a separate, strided (non-vectorizable) shared-memory load, even though this load happens far more often (once per every output point, 81×64 times) than the one-time staging load.

I restructure the shared-memory weight layout so `cc` (output-channel-within-tile) is the **fastest-varying/contiguous** dimension. This lets the hot compute loop use 128-bit (`float4`) vectorized shared-memory loads, cutting shared-memory instruction count by 4x for the register-blocked channel loop and improving memory-level parallelism for the dominant workload.

