node 1_20260821-231901 codegen_edits llm_response: Looking at this kernel, it's a depthwise convolution with a K×1 kernel (convolving only along H). The current implementation, despite staging weights in shared memory, re-reads each input element from global memory independently for every output pixel — since the CTA tiling flattens (H,W) into a single index and processes ~1 row per block, there's no reuse of overlapping input rows across the K-tap sliding window in the H dimension. This is the classic "neighboring outputs repeatedly reload reusable input activations from global memory" red flag.

**Optimization**: Restructure the kernel with CTA-level tiling over (channel, output-width) and loop internally over output-height in tiles, staging the required overlapping input rows into shared memory once per H-tile. This converts the memory access pattern from O(Hout·K) reads per (n,c,ow) column down to nearly O(H) reads (staging halo regions), directly exploiting the sliding-window reuse along H.

