node 7_20260822-010534 codegen_edits llm_response: Looking at this kernel, it's a flash-attention style implementation with GEMM-like QK^T/PV computation. The kernel already tiles K/V into shared memory, but only reuses each K/V tile across `ROWS_PER_BLOCK=8` query rows per CTA. This means K and V are re-read from global memory `N/ROWS_PER_BLOCK` times per (batch,head) — for N=256, that's re-reading K/V 32 times per head. This is the dominant structural inefficiency (poor CTA-level tiling on the M dimension), directly matching optimization principle #1 (Threadblock-level tiling for M) for GEMM-like kernels.

I'll increase `ROWS_PER_BLOCK` from 8 to 32, which quadruples reuse of each shared-memory K/V tile per global-memory load, cutting global memory traffic for K/V by 4x while keeping the same correctness structure (THREADS scales accordingly, shared memory footprint per block_n row unchanged).

