node 8_20260822-011054 codegen_edits llm_response: Looking at this flash-attention-style kernel, I identified it as a GEMM-like kernel (Q·Kᵀ and P·V). The key structural weakness is that shared-memory reads of `sK_t`/`sV_t` use the pattern `lane * epl + i`, which for `epl=32` (D=1024) gives a per-lane stride of 64 bytes in shared memory. This causes severe (16-way) shared-memory bank conflicts on every K/V float4 load, since many lanes map to the same starting bank.

I apply optimization #5 (shared-memory bank conflict reduction via layout/index permutation): remap the per-lane element indices so that within each 8-half chunk, consecutive lanes access consecutive shared-memory addresses (matching natural vectorized coalesced access), which reduces bank conflicts down to the minimum required by the 16-byte-per-thread transaction width. The same permutation is consistently applied to the initial Q load and final O store so per-thread register indexing (`q_reg[i]`/`o_reg[i]`) still matches correctly, preserving full numerical correctness.

